#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114990 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115357 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4312362-9961-5bf7-9423-42f4c2f7a13c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Area of a Triangle Given an Angle and the Length of a Leg"}] :question/id #uuid "33326641-c2fa-53db-8ff5-8dedde2a16b1" :question/math-academy-id "e-3274" :question/problem "In square units, what is the area of $\\triangle ABC$ shown below?\n\n![](images/6b/6b6385bfeb341402b8a9bcc3668a095c7dcf82cff111273275598a971e0d156b.png)" :question/worked-solution "In this case, the area of the right triangle is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A = \\dfrac 1 2 AC \\cdot BC.\n$$\n\nNotice that relative to the given angle $\\angle B,$ we have the length of the opposite side $\\overline{AC}$ and need to determine the length of the adjacent side $\\overline{BC}.$ Hence, we can use the tangent ratio to solve for $BC{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(m∠B) & =\\frac{AC}{BC} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡({60}^{∘}) & =\\frac{9}{BC} \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & =\\frac{9}{BC} \\\\ \\sqrt{3}BC & =9 \\\\ BC & =\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of $\\triangle{ABC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 9\\cdot (3\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{27\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114991 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115358 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "51739685-2d53-5c9b-870b-b557ffdf2768" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Area of a Triangle by Solving for a Variable Given a Trigonometric Ratio of One Angle"}] :question/id #uuid "60a0dba7-3007-5958-987a-d262091813cb" :question/math-academy-id "e-3277" :question/problem "Given that $\\tan(m\\angle{B}) =\\dfrac{4}{3},$ calculate the area (in square units) of $\\triangle ABC$ shown below.\n\n![](images/ac/ac4681632fd3f38f6d61a15fada02054eef264c37aaf6039465e93fc3954dbf5.png)" :question/worked-solution "In this case, the area of the right triangle is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = \\dfrac12 BC \\cdot AC.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $\\tan(m\\angle{B}) = \\dfrac{4}{3}.$ Notice that relative to $\\angle{B},$ we have expressions for the lengths of the opposite side $\\overline{AC}$ and adjacent side $\\overline{BC}.$ Hence, we can use the tangent ratio to solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(m∠B) & =\\frac{AC}{BC} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3} & =\\frac{2x+2}{2x} \\\\ 4\\cdot 2x & =3\\cdot (2x+2) \\\\ 8x & =6x+6 \\\\ 2x & =6 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the side lengths are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC & =2x+2=2(3)+2=8, \\\\ BC & =2x=2(3)=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of $\\triangle{ABC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}BC\\cdot AC \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 6\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114992 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115359 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "437a71fe-92e0-58e3-9f49-6560b2755776" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Area of a Triangle Given a Trigonometric Ratio of an Angle and the Length of a Leg"}] :question/id #uuid "514f238c-bac6-516a-a498-d1569db22471" :question/math-academy-id "e-3279" :question/problem "Given that $\\sin \\theta = \\dfrac 13,$ calculate the area (in square units) of $\\triangle ABC$ shown below.\n\n![](images/3a/3acaffefa93ecbf278a6126661fb9b128ed4f904a22306acd32289eef46d2e0a.png)" :question/worked-solution "In this case, the area of the right triangle is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = \\dfrac12 AC \\cdot BC.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $\\sin \\theta = \\dfrac13,$ where $\\theta = m\\angle{A}.$ Notice we're also given the length of the side $\\overline{BC}$ opposite to $\\angle{A}.$ Hence, we can use the sine ratio to find the length of the hypotenuse $\\overline{AB}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{BC}{AB} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & =\\frac{5}{AB} \\\\ AB & =5(3) \\\\ AB & =15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use the Pythagorean theorem to find the missing side $AC\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC & =\\sqrt{A{B}^{2}-B{C}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{15}^{2}-{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{225-25} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{200} \\\\  & =10\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of $\\triangle{ABC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}AC\\cdot BC \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 10\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =25\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114993 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115360 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe83dedc-3034-5e3a-b546-dd57c0ccc6e8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Area of a Triangle by Solving for a Variable Given an Angle"}] :question/id #uuid "759db275-8647-5bf1-a3fb-dc014c920279" :question/math-academy-id "e-3282" :question/problem "In square units rounded to one decimal place, find the area of $\\triangle ABC$ shown below.\n\n![](images/60/6049cd70d2bd6b269cead37481bcebc8403f35d90d4fc2cbb07d162243015d00.png)" :question/worked-solution "In this case, the area of the right triangle is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = \\dfrac12 AC \\cdot BC.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $m\\angle{B} = 23^\\circ.$ Notice that relative to $\\angle{B},$ we have expressions for the lengths of the opposite side $\\overline{AC}$ and adjacent side $\\overline{BC}.$ Hence, we can use the tangent ratio to solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(m∠B) & =\\frac{AC}{BC} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡({23}^{∘}) & =\\frac{x-1}{2x} \\\\ 2x\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡({23}^{∘}) & =x-1 \\\\ 1 & =x-2x\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡({23}^{∘}) \\\\ 1 & =x(1-2\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡({23}^{∘})) \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{1-2\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡({23}^{∘})} \\\\ x & =6.620\\,3…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the side lengths are \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =2x=2(6.620\\,3…)=13.240\\,6…, \\\\ AC & =x-1=(6.620\\,3…)-1=5.620\\,3….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of $\\triangle{ABC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}(5.620\\,3…)(13.240\\,6…) \\\\  & =37.208\\,0… \\\\  & ≈37.2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the one decimal place."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290613 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115357 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4312362-9961-5bf7-9423-42f4c2f7a13c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Area of a Triangle Given an Angle and the Length of a Leg"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2fe4f46-2860-5e90-a0f5-dd0dfdff62ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289214}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2fe4f46-2860-5e90-a0f5-dd0dfdff62ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289214} :answer-field/id #uuid "c02a298a-23be-5752-b8d4-70af89833980" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290146}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6710d2e0-8c0d-5c72-9f96-112ffd2507b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-251064" :question/problem "![](images/fb/fbccea4652fde939777bd69f621497f9e63ee79c7d4e4f0a5327bb141ce021ca.png)\n\nIn square units rounded to the nearest integer, the area of $△ABC$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "In this case, the area of a right triangle is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}AC\\cdot BC.\n$$\n\nNotice that relative to the given angle $∠B,$ we have the length of the opposite side $\\overset{―}{AC}$ and need to determine the length of the adjacent side $\\overset{―}{BC}.$ Hence, we can use the tangent ratio to solve for $BC:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(m∠B) & =\\frac{AC}{BC} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡({70}^{∘}) & =\\frac{6}{BC} \\\\ BC\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡({70}^{∘}) & =6 \\\\ BC & =\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡({70}^{∘})} \\\\ BC & =2.183…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of $△ABC$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 6\\cdot (2.183…) \\\\  & =6.551… \\\\  & ≈7,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115676 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115358 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "51739685-2d53-5c9b-870b-b557ffdf2768" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Area of a Triangle by Solving for a Variable Given a Trigonometric Ratio of One Angle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1757e5f4-54b5-5159-9ab2-49bce96f3df1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293023} {:answer/id #uuid "9cbbe909-d185-59d0-b912-c153b5d3e71b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293549} {:answer/id #uuid "a10e5fb3-4c1c-5b66-89cd-0598cd19bb77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293566} {:answer/id #uuid "a838bda5-a5e8-5f6a-a576-b0a05239ea5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293599} {:answer/id #uuid "ac38d79d-d749-555c-aea9-60b199533b57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293606}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1757e5f4-54b5-5159-9ab2-49bce96f3df1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293023} :answer-field/id #uuid "331c0849-08b7-58b1-a991-ead4ea6bc11c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294107}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f0a77aee-e012-5d50-a91c-7eccfeb5ef38" :question/math-academy-id "q-28266" :question/problem "![](images/48/48cb6a248ab3ce706f26dc2e84e2feb337d509041c8b9756ea32fb3817aa097f.png)\n\nGiven that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(m∠A)=5,$ calculate the area (in square units) of $△ABC$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "In this case, the area of the right triangle is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}AC\\cdot BC.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(m∠A)=5.$ Notice that relative to $∠A,$ we have expressions for the lengths of the opposite side $\\overset{―}{BC}$ and adjacent side $\\overset{―}{AC}.$ Hence, we can use the tangent ratio to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(m∠A) & =\\frac{BC}{AC} \\\\ 5 & =\\frac{2x+4}{x-1} \\\\ 5(x-1) & =2x+4 \\\\ 5x-5 & =2x+4 \\\\ 3x & =9 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the side lengths are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =2x+4=2(3)+4=10, \\\\ AC & =x-1=3-1=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of $△ABC$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}AC\\cdot BC \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 10 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345257 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115360 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe83dedc-3034-5e3a-b546-dd57c0ccc6e8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Area of a Triangle by Solving for a Variable Given an Angle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bf4f2c6-e858-51a3-b77f-acea654a35f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342344} {:answer/id #uuid "2bc114cd-0df0-534c-ba36-3dd6a784cf09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342606} {:answer/id #uuid "c8380568-73d7-53e3-af05-8aaa58f44dc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343996} {:answer/id #uuid "d67a93e4-9882-5722-89df-66236d1d4508" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344118} {:answer/id #uuid "e76db831-3092-5d07-84ac-ff8e7bc82ae9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344269}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0bf4f2c6-e858-51a3-b77f-acea654a35f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342344} :answer-field/id #uuid "58240093-25e2-5d85-9c44-cac49372812e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344640}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d12df77b-63bd-5d54-b7fd-31d2b38ec3a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-76775" :question/problem "![](images/ab/ab0e485908d86147b50e80f4d45c7e9966ab3453eb3ea01f6050bf1f7d88e2c8.png)\n\nFind the area (in square units) of $△ABC$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "In this case, the area of the right triangle is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}AB\\cdot BC.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $m∠A={60}^{∘}.$ Notice that relative to $∠A,$ we have expressions for the lengths of the opposite side $\\overset{―}{BC}$ and adjacent side $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ Hence, we can use the tangent ratio to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(m∠A) & =\\frac{BC}{AB} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡({60}^{∘}) & =\\frac{x+2}{\\sqrt{3}\\,x} \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & =\\frac{x+2}{\\sqrt{3}\\,x} \\\\ \\sqrt{3}(\\sqrt{3}\\,x) & =x+2 \\\\ 3x & =x+2 \\\\ 2x & =2 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the side lengths are \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =x+2=1+2=3, \\\\ AB & =\\sqrt{3}\\,x=\\sqrt{3}(1)=\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of $△ABC$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot AB\\cdot BC \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{3}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
